# Fetched data is REPRODUCIBLE — re-downloadable from the authority — so the big ones stay
# out of git. Everything under source/ is a fetch away; everything under ../curated/ is not
# and is tracked deliberately.
#
# These patterns used to be bare names (`temp_bc_data/`, `*.gpkg`) and did not survive the
# move into source/: hydat.sqlite3 (1.2 GB) and 2,600 bathymetry PDFs were staged in one
# `git add`. Anchored and explicit now.
source/temp_bc_data/
source/bathymetry_pdfs/
source/hydat.sqlite3
source/*.gpkg
source/*.pmtiles

# Run timing: the three Explorer pages beside it ARE tracked — they are the verbatim vendor
# source a build was made from, and `fishing_synopsis.pdf` sets that precedent. The vector
# tile cache is not: 748 binary tiles that `pipeline.runtiming.fetch.boundaries` re-fetches
# in about a minute, and that nothing reads except the polygon stitcher.
source/runtiming/cu_tiles/
*.gpkg
*.pmtiles

# Generated: candidates awaiting promotion, cleaned municipal layers. Regenerable by
# definition — the promoted answers live in ../curated/.
# `generated/*`, not `generated/` — git never descends into an excluded DIRECTORY, so a
# `!generated/README.md` under a bare `generated/` rule is silently dead. Excluding the
# CONTENTS instead leaves the directory itself visible, which is what makes the negations
# below actually apply.
generated/*

# TWO EXCEPTIONS, and they are temporary. These predate their matchers being wired, so they
# CANNOT currently be regenerated — and between them they hold 2,265 bathymetry identifiers
# already resolved to lakes, 2,122 of them name-verified. Losing them would mean re-deriving
# work nobody has the tooling to re-derive yet.
#
# When pipeline/bathymetry/generate/match.py and pipeline/stocking/generate/match.py land,
# these become ordinary regenerable candidates and these two lines come out.
!generated/bathymetry/audit.json
!generated/stocking/identifier_audit.json

# A THIRD EXCEPTION, and this one is an INPUT. `regs/sections.json` is which rules fall on which
# stretch of which water — what `corpus.sections()` loads and the whole table layer is built on.
# It was tracked before, inside a 7.7 MB design page that two modules parsed at import; moving it
# to a data file must not quietly untrack it, or a fresh clone has no table layer and the suite
# fails with FileNotFoundError. Regenerable from the bundle by pipeline/tools/build_section_data.py,
# but the bundle is not tracked either, so the file has to be.
# Open the directory, re-exclude its contents, then admit the one file — the same three-step
# the `generated/*` comment above explains, applied one level down. `!generated/regs/` alone
# would expose everything else written there (dfo_salmon/, scratch dumps).
!generated/regs/
generated/regs/*
!generated/regs/sections.json

# The tree's own documentation — the map of what each subdirectory is and who writes it.
# It belongs beside the thing it describes, not only in pipeline/docs/.
!generated/README.md
# gov.bc.ca's own copies of the synopsis chapters, fetched for the print cross-check;
# the tests skip (naming the file) when they are absent. See quota_print.OFFICIAL_DIR.
source/official/
